\paragraph{Background}
Recognizing structured text is a critical challenge with broad practical applications. While end-to-end neural sequence-to-sequence models have achieved significant improvements, they often fail to produce text that complies with the required specifications. Conversely, traditional rule-based systems offer strict adherence to constraints but lack flexibility. To reconcile the strict requirements of structured text with the flexibility inherent in natural language, Yao et al. (2019) proposed integrating context-free grammars into attention-based attention models. This approach combines the strict nature of grammars with the soft capabilities of neural models. They evaluated performance using the \emph{F1} metric, which balances precision and recall, and reported results on the ICDAR'17 Challenge. Subsequently, Li et al. (2020) proposed Gram2Seq, offering theoretical foundations and construction methods for weighted context-free grammars. Like the work of Yao et al. (2019), it combines generative and constraint properties within an encoder-decoder framework. To further refine the search process and enhance both efficiency and accuracy, Liang et al. (2020) proposed the Bottom-Up Beam Search algorithm for decoding according to a given context-free grammar. To fairly compare all methods against the Ground Truth, Liang et al. (2020) introduced the ICDAR 2020 Challenge. To date, the record score is 0.611, achieved by Liang et al. (2020) using Bottom-Up Beam Search.

