Current state-of-the-art video feature generation methods typically depend on key frames to predict feature maps, a strategy that often leads to two main issues: a significant performance gap compared to other video representation techniques and a high reliance on key frames. To address these challenges, we introduce a novel algorithm capable of utilizing short video videolets instead of key frames to design each feature representation. Our method is built upon the principle that low-level features in early network layers should be extracted from videolets, while high-level features in later layers can be extracted from single frames. Based on this intuition, we developed a novel general feature generation algorithm suitable for various video tasks. Extensive experiments on multiple benchmarks confirm the effectiveness of our approach compared to existing methods. Furthermore, our method is more adaptable to low-bandwidth networks, a crucial factor for practical applications. This work underscores the importance of tailored feature generation in video understanding systems. Code and data are available at https://github.com/yl2702/FeatureGeneration.

In this paper, we address the challenge of feature generation in video understanding. Existing methods generally fall into two categories: those based on key frames and those based on hand-crafted features. The key frame approach selects representative frames to predict feature maps, often resulting in a performance gap relative to other video representations. Conversely, hand-crafted methods require extensive human effort to select videolets for each feature, leading to suboptimal choices. To overcome these limitations, we propose a novel feature generation algorithm designed to extract appropriate features efficiently without relying solely on key frames or dependent on manual selection.

