\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

Many real-world graphs, such as social networks, do not exist in isolation but are part of a sequence of overlapping graphs sharing the same vertex set. The specific domains of these graphs—whether defined by explicit attributes or implicit graph-level patterns—are vital for understanding their dynamics. Capturing these dynamics allows us to distinguish between graphs that vary merely in appearance and those that exhibit fundamentally different behaviors.

Existing approaches to temporal patterns typically rely on implicit clustering of graphs, a method that is computationally expensive and impractical for large datasets. Conversely, metadata providing explicit grouping is rare in large, real-world changing graphs. Given these limitations, there is a clear need for a method to detect the temporal ranges and identify graph scopes.

\subsection{Contribution}

We present a novel temporal pattern embedding embedding framework that requires no temporal patterns to be learned, provided that graph scopes over time are given as input. We demonstrate that incorporating scope embeddings dramatically boosts performance on various metrics and graphs, even when no distinct patterns exist and graphs appear random.

