We propose a novel task and approach for estimating the 3D pose of moving objects using single-view last frames, a scenario that is both practically and theoretically significant yet challenging due to the absence of prior object information and the presence of motion blur. Current methods relying on appearance or template matching are ineffective for unknown objects, while template-based methods for transparent objects fail in the last frame due to severe motion blur. We present a new generative framework that leverages class-conditional information to recover missing features and utilizes a novel alignment strategy to correct misalignments caused by motion. Experiments across diverse scenarios indicate that our method outperforms previous state-of-the-art approaches on moving objects. We further validate the method's effectiveness for both unknown and transparent objects, achieving superior results compared to techniques designed for static objects without motion blur. We demonstrate strong generalization across object categories and across conditions varying in prior information and motion blur. To support this work, we introduce a new dataset of 3D annotations for 10 objects derived from driving videos. Source code is available at https://github.com/SeongJinLee97/LateFramePose.
