\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

\paragraph{Non-stationary Decision Making} Numerous operational decision problems exhibit temporal variations in their distributional parameters, a phenomenon known as non-stationarity. Examples include non-stochastic queueing theory \citep{van2019non}, dynamic pricing \citep{feng2007dynamic}, inventory management \citep{feng2007dynamic}, and non-stochastic routing \citep{feng2007dynamic}. Given the critical nature of these problems, there is a pressing need for efficient algorithms capable of generating high-quality policies within reasonable timeframes.

